# WARDEN — Fraud Analyst Desk

Two pieces:

- **`app.py`** — Flask API: JWT auth, transaction scoring, SHAP explainability,
  metrics (confusion matrix, ROC/PR, model comparison), CSV/PDF export, admin
  endpoints, optional Socket.IO real-time push.
- **`dashboard.html`** — the frontend. Open it directly in a browser (double-click
  the file, no build step). It talks to the API at `http://localhost:5000/api`.
  If the API isn't running, it automatically falls back to an offline demo mode
  with simulated data so you can still click through everything.

## Run the backend

```bash
cd warden-backend
pip install -r requirements.txt
python app.py
```

This seeds a SQLite file `warden.db` with two accounts on first run:

| Email | Password | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `admin@warden.local` | `Warden123!` | admin |
| `analyst@warden.local` | `Warden123!` | analyst |

By default it uses a heuristic risk scorer (no model file needed) so it runs
out of the box. It also seeds 15 sample transactions and starts scoring new
ones every few seconds once the dashboard is open.

## Enable the AI narrative + chat assistant

These features call Gemini through your own backend (matching your original
notebook), so they need a Gemini API key on the server:

```bash
export GEMINI_API_KEY=your-key-here
python app.py
```

Get a free key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey. If you landed on a
specific model in your notebook after debugging quota/deprecation issues,
set it explicitly (defaults to `gemini-2.0-flash` otherwise):

```bash
export GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.0-flash   # or whichever model you settled on
```

Without `GEMINI_API_KEY` set, the narrative and chat panels show a clear
"not configured" message instead of failing silently.

## Wire up your real model

Point it at the artifacts from your notebook:

```bash
export WARDEN_MODEL_PATH=/path/to/xgb_model.pkl
export WARDEN_DATA_PATH=/path/to/X_test.csv      # optional: real ROC/PR + confusion matrix
export WARDEN_LABELS_PATH=/path/to/y_test.csv    # single column of true labels, same row order as X_test
python app.py
```

`X_test.csv` needs these columns (matching your feature engineering step):
`step, amount, oldbalanceOrg, newbalanceOrig, oldbalanceDest, newbalanceDest, errorBalanceOrig, errorBalanceDest, origAccountEmptied`.

Without a model file, `/api/metrics/confusion-matrix` and `/api/metrics/curve`
return the numbers you already reported from the notebook (F1 0.989 for
XGBoost, etc.) so the ML dashboard still looks correct.

## Open the dashboard

Just open `dashboard.html` in a browser. Sign in with one of the seeded
accounts, then verify with the on-screen demo OTP code (no real SMS is sent —
this is a prototype 2FA step).

## What's real vs. simulated

**Real and functional:**
- JWT auth, registration, account lockout after 5 failed logins, audit log, login history
- Flask endpoints for transactions, decisions, metrics, CSV/PDF export
- SHAP explainability (real SHAP values if you wire up your model, heuristic otherwise)
- AI narrative + chat assistant — calls the Gemini API server-side, grounded in
  the selected transaction's data and SHAP values
- CSV export (server-generated); PDF export (server-generated via `fpdf2`)

**Simulated / for demo purposes:**
- Transaction locations and "impossible travel" detection use mock city
  coordinates, not real IP geolocation or device fingerprinting
- Real-time feed generates synthetic transactions on a timer rather than
  ingesting live payment traffic
- Email alerts aren't wired up (browser notifications + a sound alert are —
  click the bell icon and allow permissions); email would need an SMTP/SES
  integration
- One-click model retraining and multi-model A/B rollout aren't included —
  those need a training pipeline and model registry beyond this scope

**Note on the AI calls:** the dashboard calls Gemini through your Flask
backend (`/api/transactions/<id>/narrative` and `/chat`), which holds
`GEMINI_API_KEY` server-side. The browser never talks to Google's API
directly, so your key is never exposed client-side.